      "text": "To find the sum of all \\( x \\) that satisfy the equation \\( f^{-1}(x) = f((2x)^{-1}) \\), we start by determining the inverse function \\( f^{-1}(x) \\) of \\( f(x) = 16x + 3 \\).\n\nThe inverse function \\( f^{-1}(x) \\) is found by solving \\( y = 16x + 3 \\) for \\( x \\):\n\\[\ny = 16x + 3 \\implies y - 3 = 16x \\implies x = \\frac{y - 3}{16}.\n\\]\nThus, the inverse function is:\n\\[\nf^{-1}(x) = \\frac{x - 3}{16}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to evaluate \\( f((2x)^{-1}) \\). First, we find the reciprocal of \\( 2x \\):\n\\[\n(2x)^{-1} = \\frac{1}{2x}.\n\\]\nThen, we apply the function \\( f \\) to \\( \\frac{1}{2x} \\):\n\\[\nf\\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) = 16 \\left( \\frac{1}{2x} \\right) + 3 = \\frac{16}{2x} + 3 = \\frac{8}{x} + 3.\n\\]\nSo, the equation \\( f^{-1}(x) = f((2x)^{-1}) \\) becomes:\n\\[\n\\frac{x - 3}{16} = \\frac{8}{x} + 3.\n\\]\nTo clear the fraction, we multiply both sides by \\( 16x \\):\n\\[\nx(x - 3) = 16 \\left( \\frac{8}{x} + 3 \\right) x \\implies x^2 - 3x = 128 + 48x \\implies x^2 - 51x - 128 = 0.\n\\]\nThis is a quadratic equation, which we can solve using the quadratic formula \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -51 \\), and \\( c = -128 \\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{51 \\pm \\sqrt{51^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-128)}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{51 \\pm \\sqrt{2601 + 512}}{2} = \\frac{51 \\pm \\sqrt{3113}}{2}.\n\\]\nThe solutions to the quadratic equation are:\n\\[\nx = \\frac{51 + \\sqrt{3113}}{2} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{51 - \\sqrt{3113}}{2}.\n\\]\nThe sum of the solutions to the quadratic equation \\( x^2 - 51x - 128 = 0 \\) is given by the coefficient of \\( x \\) with a sign change, which is 51. Therefore, the sum of all \\( x \\) that satisfy the original equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{51}.\n\\]",
