      "text": "Let $f^{-1}(x)$ be the inverse of $f(x)$. Then $f(f^{-1}(x))=x$. Therefore, $f^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-3}{16}$. We are given the equation $f^{-1}(x)=f((2x)^{-1})$. Substituting the expressions, we get $\\frac{x-3}{16}=\\frac{(2x)^{-1}-3}{16}$. Simplifying, we get $x-3=\\frac{1}{2x}-3$. Let $y=2x$, so we have $y-3=\\frac{1}{y}-3$. Multiply both sides by $y$, we get $y^2-3y-1=0$. Using the quadratic formula, we find the solutions to be $y=\\frac{3 \\pm\\sqrt{13}}{2}$. Reverting back to $x$, we have $2x=\\frac{3 \\pm\\sqrt{13}}{2}$, so $x=\\frac{3 \\pm\\sqrt{13}}{4}$. The sum of the solutions is $\\boxed{\\frac{3+\\sqrt{13}}{4}+\\frac{3-\\sqrt{13}}{4}}= \\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}$.\nThe answer is: \\frac{3}{2}",
